From f527c0afc80bac25230215fc86810d411d168296 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aaron <blue@aquarat.co.uk>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 15:46:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(ml): apply ggml patches when building libclipvit

Adds machine-learning/ggml/patches, whose *.patch files are applied to the
ggml source tree before it is compiled, by patches/apply.cmake. CMakeLists
invokes it on both source paths: directly before add_subdirectory() for
CLIPVIT_GGML_DIR, and as the PATCH_COMMAND of the FetchContent fallback.
Patch paths are relative to the ggml tree root, so one file is valid for a
standalone ggml checkout and for llama.cpp's ggml subtree alike, and applying
is idempotent, so a patch already carried by the tree is skipped rather than
failing the build and a landed patch can simply be deleted.

patch(1) is preferred over "git apply": when the ggml tree is a subdirectory
of a larger repository, git resolves the patch paths against the repository
root rather than the working directory and exits successfully having changed
nothing. The result is re-checked after applying so any such silent no-op is
a configure-time error instead of a quietly unpatched library.

The first patch hoists the A-tile load out of the coopmat mul_mm inner loop,
so each operand tile is fetched once per k step instead of cms_per_row times.
On a Vulkan driver that does not eliminate redundant shared-memory loads this
takes ViT-H-14-378 from 1519 to 1400 ms, and the CLIP image endpoint from
1537 to 1448 ms, with byte-identical embeddings.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
